// Survival: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== survival.f01 | feature | three-act
title: The Seamstress Of The Ice
genre: survival + historical-drama
tone: White, spare and stubbornly practical
era: 1921 | period
setting: An uninhabited island in the Chukchi Sea | real | island
logline: An Inupiaq seamstress hired to sew for four explorers claiming an Arctic island becomes, when the relief ship fails to come, the only one who knows how to stay alive, though nobody taught her to hunt.
premise: Four young men have a flag, a theory that the Arctic is friendly, and supplies for six months. {ada} was hired in Nome to make their fur clothes because she needs money to bring her son home from a children's hospital. She is afraid of polar bears and of guns. The ship that is to fetch them next summer is turned back by ice.
synopsis: {ada} is hired for a job, not an adventure, and the men treat her as staff. The first winter goes by the leader's book. When the ice does not open and no sail comes, the book runs out. {crawford}, who leads, decides three of them will walk seven hundred miles across the frozen sea to Siberia for help; the fourth, {knight}, has scurvy and stays with {ada}. The three are never seen again. The second act is two people in a tent, one dying and resentful, one teaching herself from nothing. {ada} was raised in a mission school; she can sew anything and has never set a trap. She learns by failing: a fox takes the bait and the trap; she builds a gun rest because the rifle knocks her down; she makes a skin boat from driftwood and canvas and loses it to a gale and makes another. {knight} tells her she is letting him starve. She chews his meat for him. She keeps a diary in pencil because a white man once told her nobody would believe her otherwise. He dies in June. The third act is {ada} alone with the expedition's cat, a platform she has built to watch for bears, and a routine: check traps, cut wood, write the page. She shoots a seal and drags it home on her own sewing. When {noice}, the relief captain, lands in August, he finds a woman who has been surviving professionally and an island the men's country will not want after all. In Nome the newspapers want a heroine or a villain. {ada} wants her wages. She collects her son.
question: Who is the explorer: the one with the flag, or the one still alive when the ship comes?
themes: competence without credit; the work of staying alive; who gets to tell it
audience: Adults and older teenagers
viewpoint: With {ada}, whose diary is the only narration
stakes: Her life, her son's place in the hospital, and the truth of what happened, which she alone can tell
conflict: A woman hired to sew against the Arctic, a dying man's contempt, and her own terror of the bear
ending: She is found alive, alone, with the cat. She takes her pay and her son and declines to be a story.
experience: Loneliness you can feel in your teeth, the satisfaction of each small skill won, and a rescue that is almost an intrusion
look: Canvas, driftwood, wide grey shingle; a figure very small on a watching platform; pencil on a ruled page
sound: Wind in guy ropes, a cat, a pencil. A hymn she half remembers.
m: survive | nature | self-vs-others | bittersweet
@ ada | Ada Tulugaq | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 23 | Her wages, for her son | He is in a hospital she cannot pay | She has never hunted and is terrified of bears | Timid by habit, relentless by necessity | From hired seamstress to the island's only competent inhabitant | Soft, mission-school English; firm in her diary | Reindeer parka of her own making, thimble on a thong
@ knight | Lorne Hadley | co-lead | antagonist | human | he/him | 28 | Not to die on this beach | He believed the leader's theory | Scurvy, and dependence on a woman he looked down on | Brave, bitter, ashamed | From contempt to something he cannot quite say | Curt; cruel when frightened | Swollen gums, a wool cap never removed
@ crawford | Allan Marsh | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 20 | To prove the island and himself | He is the youngest leader anyone has heard of | The plan was his | Earnest, certain, unlucky | From commander to three tracks going south-west | Hearty; speaks of "the party" | New furs, the flag folded in his pack
@ noice | Captain Harold Voss | featured | ally | human | he/him | 35 | To bring them off, and the credit with them | He was late | There is one person on the beach and it is not who he came for | Efficient, self-interested, fair enough | From rescuer to a man handed a diary | Brisk sailor's talk | Sealskin coat, a camera
~ ada - knight | seamstress and the man left with her | servant and master | nurse and patient, and neither will name it
~ ada - crawford | hired hand and leader | orders | a note he leaves her with the rifle
~ ada - noice | survivor and rescuer | he looks past her for the men | she hands him the diary and asks about her pay
